    {
      "id": 152610,
      "name": "Agent Bon",
      "username": "Agent_Bon",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/13edae/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2016-01-08T08:07:16.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Some of the original cards have special interactions with Nemesis cards and/or Device cards.&nbsp; Mix-and-match can impact how effective those are.&nbsp; Nothing wrong with throttling them down a little bit if you choose though.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For reference, most of the original Vengeance decks had 5 Nemesis cards.&nbsp; I know Baron Blade had cards that interacted with Nemesis cards, not sure about the rest (could have sworn someone else did too...).&nbsp; Some did it indirectly by affecting all Villain targets in some way.&nbsp; I think 3? of them had Devices (Baron Blade, Friction, Ermine)?&nbsp; BB had more Devices and more cards that interacted with Devices than the rest.&nbsp; I don't have my Vengeance on-hand atm.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>None of the Villains decks have Device cards, but some have Nemesis cards:</p><p>Plague Rat: 0</p><p>The Operative: 3</p><p>Sergeant Steel: 0</p><p>Miss Information: 4</p><p>La Capitan: 0</p><p>Greazer: 3</p><p>Citizens Hammer and Anvil: 0</p><p>Bugbear: 3</p><p>Biomancer: 0</p><p>Ambuscade: 5</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Also, Plague Rat, Sergeant Steel, and Biomancer have pleanty of minions without Nemesis cards.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>On a related note about mixing, playing the Festival environment with Vengeance/Nemesis could result in a lot of counting and fiddling.&nbsp; Personally, I think my regular group would prefer to only play it with normal villains, but some people don't mind the overhead as much.&nbsp; It would be a really unusual game.</p>",
      "post_number": 4,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:31:48.505Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
